es by using a fluorescent probe, Zinquin: relationship to metallothionein and intracellular zinc</article-title><source>J Biochem</source><volume>303</volume><fpage>781</fpage><lpage>786</lpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b12-bmi-2007-107"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Coyle</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Philcox</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Rofe</surname><given-names>AM</given-names></name></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Metallothionein induction in cultured rat hepatocytes by arthritic rat serum, activated macrophages, interleukin-6, interleukin-11 and leukaemia inhibitory factor</article-title><source>Inflamm Res</source><volume>44</volume><fpage>475</fpage><lpage>481</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8597881</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b13-bmi-2007-107"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Downing</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Downing</surname><given-names>WL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1992</year><article-title>Spatial aggregation, precision, and power in surveys of freshwater mussel populations</article-title><source>Can J Fish Aquat Sci</source><volume>49</volume><fpage>985</fpage><lpage>991</lpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b14-bmi-2007-107"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dragun</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Erk</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Raspor</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Ivankovic</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Pavicic</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Metal and metallothionein level in the heat-treated cytosol of gills of transplanted mussels <italic>Mytilus galloprovinciallis</italic> Lmk</article-title><source>Environ Internat</source><volume>30</volume><fpage>1019</fpage><lpage>1025</lpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b15-bmi-2007-107"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fujimoto</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Sakuma</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Inoue</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Uno</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Fujita</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>The endocrine disruptor nonylphenol preferentially blocks cyclooxygenase-1</article-title><source>Life Sci</source><volume>70</volume><fpage>2209</fpage><lpage>2214</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12005180</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b16-bmi-2007-107"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Katakai</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Nakajima</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Keefer</surname><given-names>LK</given-names></name><name><surname>Waalkes</surname><given-names>MP</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Nitric oxide induces metallothionein (MT) gene expression apparently by displacing zinc bound to MT</article-title><source>Toxicol Lett</source><volume>119</volume><fpage>103</fpage><lpage>108</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11311571</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b17-bmi-2007-107"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gagn&#x000e9;</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Marion</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Denizeau</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><year>1990</year